Shadow business secretary Jonathan  Reynolds with us now we can't afford  these pay Rises that the unions are  asking for well let's unpack this a  little bit now when ministers say they  can't afford it there's a little bit of  Truth in that because the economy hasn't  done very well for 12 years under them  and so any resources we've got to spend  on public services are diminished on  what they should be so like what they  should add to their sentences there is  if we can't afford it it's their fault  to do so however there are things  government can do what I worry listening  to those collection of cabinet ministers  there is  they think they've got an opportunity  here to deflect from the fact that they  haven't run the economy in public  services very well and they can somehow  blame working people or unions by  highlighting the strikes as the strikes  of the symptom of how badly they have  run things and particularly on the NHS  you know everyone should recognize the  pay issue is also about workload and  burden on AHS staff and there are things  the government could do they could for  instance take our plan which is a future  labor government would have abandon the  the non-dom rule the special uh tax  regime for the super everything well but  it would pay in this case what we would  use the money for for a significant  expansion of the NHS Workforce in terms  of more doctors more District Health  visitors more midwives more nurses that  would help a great deal and I think you  just if you're a government Minister  you've got to roll up your sleeves and  try and resolve these disputes nobody  nobody wants to take industrial action  they don't want to lose the money but  they also don't want to see the impact  on the public but if the government  won't listen to them what option of  people got but to try and make  themselves heard by taking that action  so there are things the government doing  it's not good enough to come on and just  be passive and say look we're somehow  commentating on this they've got to get  involved and resolve these disputes so  you'd use the non-dom money um for um  pay uh in the National Health Service  and others what about the Eco plans that  you have got to make us all a little bit  Greener at home how would you pay for  that well those plans are about  investment spending so where we would  increase Revenue spending for instance  on the on the NHS we will always  identify the results it will all  government spending Capital spending is  borrowing and the question is is the  return you get for doing that greater  than the cost of of investing so I think  the case on climate change is absolutely  clear that the costs of not doing  anything are far more extensive than  taking action but crucially what you get  with our plans our ability to get the  green jobs of the future the good  skilled work that's available whether  that's in new nuclear or new Renewables  or Energy Efficiency measures in  people's homes and get the benefit of  that this could be for the UK the  biggest Economic Opportunity for many  many years and we don't want to be  following the rest of we want to be  leading we want to be a clean energy  superpower exporting that power  exporting the technology and The  Innovation that we've got in the UK but  the government's got a lead on that and  so you would borrow more than the  government's is planning sparrower than  if you were in power well  this government borrows money for  instance to pay for tax Goods  the test of borrowing is about whether  you're investing it and you'll get a  greater return from investing it than  you would from not taking that action  now I don't think anyone can seriously  say we shouldn't respond to Net Zero we  shouldn't for instance make sure that we  are in line with our International  commitments but what we want to do is  get the maximum economic benefit for the  UK of doing that so give me one specific  example you know this country has about  three quarters of a million people  working in the automotive industry so  it's a jewel in the crown of our  industrial base if we do not invest to  make sure we've got battery production  for electric vehicles in the UK we won't  just filter seize that opportunity we'll  lose those existing jobs and that  requires the government investing a  little bit of money to make sure there's  a greater amount of private investment  coming in that's how other countries are  doing that's why Germany is so far ahead  of the UK for instance so it's about  making sure you're you're clearly  identifying the opportunities and if you  need to invest some government money to  do that customer is greater yes but it's  not about borrowing more money for  day-to-day spending that's where this  government went wrong and what should we  do about onshore wind farms just to  underline I think labor are in favor of  more  um but we're hearing that it's it's an  issue that is splitting this government  some of them are a little bit NIMBY and  some of them are saying no no it's what  we need what would you say to the  government this morning well we've been  making the case for quite a few years  now since they had the de facto ban an  onshore wind and it makes no sense to  ban the cheapest form of energy  production I mean as it stands light on  the landscape says David Davis well that  isn't what the public see this as every  single pool shows that actually a lot of  conservative and bees are out of step  with the public on this but to have a  position where you completely ban the  cheapest form of energy production even  if people are very much in favor in area  or if it's a particularly appropriate  thing I actually think winforms look  very attractive I'm used to seeing them  on the landscape they make a quite a  wonderful contribution to that but most  of all if you look at how much how much  damage has been done by this energy  crisis where you know the price of  fossil fuels has absolutely spiked the  idea you don't try and reduce your  dependency on that volatility you don't  see the opportunity you don't use safe  secure clean forms of energy in the UK  how can that make sense and as ever what  we see with this government is no matter  what the prime minister or any  government ministers might come on and  say every issue is determined by the  internal politics of the conservative  party by the divisions they've got and  it's just nowhere to run a country  should we be sending um albanians who  are seeking Asylum back the moment that  they've stepped foot on British soil  look where we have a legal entry into  the UK what we've got to have is a  system that resolves those claims  on the time scale that people quite  rightly expect and which we need I think  we've got the worst of all Worlds at the  minute where people who are who should  be being granted asylum in the UK aren't  getting an answer clearly enough and  people who should be removed aren't me  being removed enough but if you've only  got a process I think this government's  two percent of the claims that were made  last year it doesn't matter what else  you do the system isn't going to work  they should take the money they're  wasting on gimmicks like Rwanda and  spend it on processing these claims  having the capacity we need and  everything else I think it's just froth  okay does that is does labor think that  Albania is a safe country  I think it's not helpful to single out  particular countries about Albania do  you think it's the same organized  criminality issues about certain  countries but I think what we've got to  acknowledge is the system as well  doesn't matter what country people are  coming from  you cannot have a system that simply  doesn't get to the bottom of whether  claims should be upheld or not and but  if it's a safe country they shouldn't be  coming from there I mean that's what the  same confusion because okay so is  Albania a safe country I think Albania  is a safe country though they shouldn't  be here so they should be said that well  I think look again focusing on you know  David the government are attempting to  try and put this on particular countries  rather than accept their own failings  that the system isn't working as it  should do so our focus should be on  demanding of this government an  efficient Fair system with the capacity  it needs to resolve these claims and and  that's nothing else will do unless the  government acknowledges their own  failings on that key issue

